{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfRZ8E7RkBWJ51fjpD6XH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 03 is done. Everything is written and verified, nothing is committed, and nothing touched `solutions/`.\n\n**Finding.** There is a hardness peak, and the kill criterion is not met. At every size from 15 to 40 customers, in both generators and at both product ratios, the median nodes to refute `optimum − 1` rise and then fall with density by two orders of magnitude each side at n = 40. The peak sits at about three customers per product when m = n and two when m = 2n, or more uniformly where the optimum is about a third of n. It sharpens with n. Along the ridge the median grows exponentially, doubling every 2.9 to 3.5 customers, and a power law fits five to ten times worse. Off the ridge at fixed density growth is still exponential but slower, doubling every 4.5 to 4.9 customers. Chu & Stuckey's \"density 2\" classes have a realised 2.7 to 2.8 customers per product, which places them in the peak cell at n = 30 and n = 40, and their density 4 is the dense shoulder. The one series that is monotone, fixed d at m = 2n, stops at the density where the Bernoulli series at the same ratio puts the peak.\n\n**Deliverables.**\n- `learning/hardness_map.py`, runnable as `python -m learning.hardness_map`, about 3 seconds. Also `--config csearch` and `--per-class` variants, both written and compared.\n- `reports/ml_nature.md` §11 with tables, the kill verdict, size range, regenerate commands, and a pre-registered extrapolation for item 06 to test against the five recorded 125×125 counts.\n- `reports/figures/hardness_map.png` and three full table files under `reports/`.\n- `tests/test_hardness_map.py`, seven tests on a synthetic campaign with a known peak, width, rate and order parameter. Full suite: 791 passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed.\n- PROGRESS.md updated to 3/6 solved with an iteration entry, and item 03 marked done in iterations.md.\n\n**One caveat for the reader.** The order-parameter result is split. Mean degree predicts the height of the surface at fixed n best, but its peak location drifts. `optimum / n` has the most constant peak location but collapses slightly worse. The report states both rather than picking one."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":494,"cache_read_input_tokens":193496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":494,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":727,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17be0253-0105-4031-b81c-e1c1ec01b2d9","uuid":"d22639dd-8919-4755-923d-2d8abcc4a943","timestamp":"2026-09-26T06:11:10.272Z","request_id":"req_011CfRZ8BomUzSrLCLS963gs"}
